  "body": "## Why\n\nThe dashboard persists its own state and restores it on reload. The desktop app persisted preferences but nothing about **the window itself** — and one line actively destroyed the memory the platform was already keeping.\n\n## The bug\n\nChromium stores window geometry per profile. A real profile this feature created holds:\n\n```\nbrowser.window_placement = {'left': 10, 'top': 10, 'right': 1410, 'bottom': 910, ...}\n```\n\n`AppModeWindow` passed `--window-size` on **every** launch, overwriting it. Resize or move the window, and the next launch discarded it.\n\nThe configured size is now the **initial** size: passed when the profile has no remembered placement, or when the configured value differs from the size last applied (recorded in `desktop.json`). So a manual resize survives indefinitely, and changing the setting is still honoured — **once** — rather than silently ignored.\n\nThe alternative considered was \"pass it unless the setting is at its default\", rejected because it compares against a *constant* rather than *history*: anyone running a non-default size would have it forced on every launch forever, permanently defeating the platform's memory.\n\nReading the placement is best-effort by construction — missing file, unreadable file, malformed JSON, or an unexpected shape all mean \"no memory yet, pass the size\". Never an exception on a launch path.\n\n## A defect caught in review\n\n`minimize_to_tray` is **off by default**, so closing the window *ends* the app — and the first implementation recorded `window_open=False` on that path too. The ordinary act of closing the app would have stopped it ever opening a window again:\n\n```\nlaunch → close → quit → relaunch → tray icon and nothing else\n```\n\nwhich looks exactly like a broken start. Now only a close the app **survives** records \"no window\"; a close that quits leaves the state alone, because the user's last intent was an app with a window. Guard added and mutation-tested — it fails by name against the original code.\n\n## The other half needed no code\n\napp-mode already launches with a dedicated `--user-data-dir`, so the dashboard's own `localStorage` — active view, filters, theme, auto-refresh — is keyed to a stable origin inside a profile that persists across launches. That half of \"persistency like the dashboard has\" was **already satisfied**; it's recorded rather than reimplemented.\n\n## Verification\n\n**Full suite: 6 failed, 4364 passed, 26 skipped** vs baseline `6 failed, 4350 passed` on unmodified `fork/main` in the same shell — same six PowerShell installer failures, **+14 tests, zero regressions**.\n\nruff format, ruff check, ty: clean, **no suppressions**. Widening the override union to include ints broke `ty`'s narrowing in `_update_state`; fixed with explicit casts after the membership checks rather than an ignore.\n\n**Guards mutation-tested, each confirmed to fail by name:**\n\n| Guard | Broken by |\n| --- | --- |\n| skip `--window-size` when placement is remembered | forcing it unconditionally |\n| tolerant read of a corrupt `Preferences` | narrowing the except clause |\n| session-restore round trip | always persisting `True` |\n| close-that-quits leaves state alone | restoring the unconditional write |\n\nThe admin endpoint carries the three new fields through, so an unrelated dashboard save cannot reset them — that regression has happened here before.\n\n## Residual risks\n\n- **No real browser window was launched.** No real profile and none of the user's `~/.fcc` was touched — every test runs against a fake profile under `tmp_path` with `HOME` redirected. That Chromium honours the *omission* of `--window-size` the way its `Preferences` file implies is documented behaviour read out of a real profile, not exercised against a live binary here.\n- **Migration nuance:** on a profile upgraded from the always-forcing code, `last_applied_*` starts unset, so the first post-upgrade launch applies the configured size once more before going hands-off. Unavoidable — the old code never recorded what it applied.\n- **Pre-existing and unchanged:** `handle_window_closed` is still not wired to an OS-level window-close event, so it is reached through the tray rather than by clicking the window's own close button.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
