    "message": "feat(desktop): remember where you put the window, and whether it was open\n\nThe dashboard persists its own state and restores it on reload. The desktop\napp persisted preferences but nothing about the window itself, and one line\nactively destroyed the memory the platform was already keeping.\n\nChromium stores window geometry per profile. A real profile this feature\ncreated holds:\n\n    browser.window_placement = {'left': 10, 'top': 10, 'right': 1410,\n                                'bottom': 910, 'maximized': False, ...}\n\nand AppModeWindow passed --window-size on EVERY launch, overwriting it. Resize\nor move the window and the next launch discarded it. The configured size is\nnow the INITIAL size: it is passed when the profile has no remembered\nplacement, or when the configured value differs from the size last applied,\nwhich is recorded in desktop.json. A manual resize therefore survives\nindefinitely, and changing the setting is still honoured -- once -- rather\nthan being silently ignored. The alternative considered was \"pass it unless\nthe setting is at its default\", rejected because it compares against a\nconstant rather than history: anyone running a non-default size would have it\nforced on every launch forever, permanently defeating the platform's memory.\n\nReading the remembered placement is best-effort by construction. A missing\nfile, unreadable file, malformed JSON, or an unexpected shape all mean \"no\nmemory yet, pass the size\" -- never an exception on a launch path.\n\nWhether a window was open is persisted and restored, so quitting with the\nwindow closed and relaunching brings back the tray-only state you left.\n\nA defect in that restore was caught in review and fixed. minimize_to_tray is\noff by default, so closing the window ENDS the app -- and the first\nimplementation recorded window_open=False on that path too. The ordinary act\nof closing the app would have stopped it ever opening a window again: launch,\nclose, quit, relaunch, and you would get a tray icon and nothing else, looking\nexactly like a broken start. Only a close the app SURVIVES now records \"no\nwindow\"; a close that quits leaves the state alone, because the user's last\nintent was an app with a window.\n\nThe other half of the request needed no code. app-mode already launches with a\ndedicated --user-data-dir, so the dashboard's own localStorage -- active view,\nfilters, theme, auto-refresh -- is keyed to a stable origin inside a profile\nthat persists across launches. That half was already satisfied; it is recorded\nhere rather than reimplemented.\n\nVerification:\n\n- Full suite: 6 failed, 4364 passed, 26 skipped, against a baseline of\n  6 failed, 4350 passed measured on unmodified fork/main in the same shell.\n  The same six PowerShell installer failures, +14 tests, zero regressions.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty: clean, with no suppressions. Widening the\n  override union to include ints broke ty's narrowing in _update_state; fixed\n  with explicit casts after the membership checks rather than an ignore.\n- Guards mutation-tested and confirmed to fail BY NAME: skipping --window-size\n  when placement is remembered, the tolerant read of a corrupt Preferences\n  file, the session-restore round trip, and the close-that-quits case.\n- The admin endpoint carries the three new fields through, so an unrelated\n  dashboard save cannot reset them. That regression has happened here before.\n\nNot verified: no real browser window was launched, and no real profile or the\nuser's own ~/.fcc was touched -- every test runs against a fake profile under\ntmp_path with HOME redirected. That Chromium honours the omission of\n--window-size the way its Preferences file implies is documented behaviour\nread from a real profile, not exercised against a live binary here.\n\nKnown and unchanged: handle_window_closed is still not wired to an OS-level\nwindow-close event, so it is reached through the tray rather than by clicking\nthe window's own close button. That gap predates this change.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
